# orca-proxy firewall-sync sudoers entry (design ticket #12).
#
# Grants the unprivileged service user NOPASSWD access to exactly one
# script, invoked with exactly one fixed set of arguments — the
# sudoers-gated helper the Management API shells out to via `sudo -n` on
# every VM create/delete (firewall.py's FirewallSync / run_reconcile_script,
# which always calls it with these same --db/--bridge/--proxy-port values
# for a given install). Pinning the arguments closes off the fact that a
# bare "NOPASSWD: /path/to/cmd" with no argument list otherwise permits
# *any* arguments — see firewall.py's _BRIDGE_NAME_RE comment for why an
# attacker-controlled --bridge would otherwise be a straight
# shell-injection-to-root primitive.
#
# __FIREWALL_BIN__ is a root-owned path outside the target user's home
# directory (install.sh installs a standalone stdlib-only copy of the
# script there) — unlike a path resolving into the user's own venv, the
# target user cannot overwrite what this rule actually executes.
#
# install.sh substitutes __USER__/__FIREWALL_BIN__/__DB_PATH__/__BRIDGE__/
# __PROXY_PORT__ before writing this to
# /etc/sudoers.d/orca-proxy-firewall-sync (mode 0440, validated with
# `visudo -c` before being trusted).

__USER__ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: __FIREWALL_BIN__ --db __DB_PATH__ --bridge __BRIDGE__ --proxy-port __PROXY_PORT__
